Archimedes Nethack 3.1.1
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
I've done a port of Nethack 3.1.1 for the Arc but I want to know if anyone
else has done it as well before I start distributing my port. I've had it
up and running for about 1 and half months, but decided to hold back
because I've just finished my final year and will be leaving Uni soon and
therefore cannot support my version when new patches are made. All
games features are in it, but some system stuff is missing (see below).
The reason for these omissions was because this is the first port I've done
and I don't really know RISC OS very well (especially since I don't have the
Programmer's Reference Manuals - I'm waiting for the RISC OS 3 ones before
I part with my cash).

System Requirements
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1. Total disk space taken up by essential files is 962662 bytes (this
   include executable) - so you either need a high density drive or a
   hard drive.
2. Executable is 610143 bytes long - so you might get away with running
   it on 1Mbyte machine.

What's In It
~~~~~~~~~~~~
1. All game features have been compiled in e.g. sinks, Elbereth etc...
   for maximum fun.
2. Full colour.
3. Uses TERMLIB - non ANSI though.
4. All printable ASCII chars can be redefined to suit - I've included a
   font set which includes chars designed by myself and some taken from
   the Arc port of Hack 2.3e by Andrew Bray.

What's Not In It
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1. No RAM DISK support.
2. No floppy support (i.e. disk swapping)
3. When you die, you have to manually go into the levels directory and
   delete all the files there. I didn't have time to write the code to
   do this automatically.
4. Doesn't run in a window in the Desktop.
5. Command line (key !) isn't implemented.
6. The Alt-key doesn't function as the Meta-key which is available on other
   computers. So to preform the action when Meta-<key> is pressed, the
   extended command list should be used via # (Hackers will know what I mean)

Technical Stuff
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1. I had to write versions of the UNIX open(), read(), write(), lseek() and
   close() - it took ages but they are nice calls. Was there are a simpler
   way? e.g. are these available from the GNU C library sources?
2. I had to change the termlib source code a bit because normally, code
   sequences finish with a terminating \0, however, 0's are sometimes
   used as data as well e.g. 31,0,2 which means cursor goto (0,2)
3. I hate bloody ADFS! A 10 character maximum filename length and only 77
   files max per directory created the most hassle in fixing. I only wished
   that there was a 12 char max so that the PC filenaming convention could
   be used ... only if ...

If you want Nethack 3.1.1, mail me with your e-mail address and I'll send
you the executables. Read the letter 'ReadMe1st' first. Please don't ask
for sources because they are on 10 disks. The extra code required for the
Arc are surrounded by the obligatory #ifdef ARC ... #endif, so I could do
a contextual diff, but I don't have the original files anymore - the
problems of having a small hard drive. I've completed the game in wizard
mode so there aren't any major bugs in it. Meanwhile... happy Hacking!

